<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The player view: the same PlayerView, with the picture drawn into a
    TextureView instead of a SurfaceView. The television's first, then the
    phone's.

    A SurfaceView shows through a hole punched in the window, and on the
    household's Chromecast the hole is only punched where a frame redraws. The
    regions that did not redraw since Home was on screen kept Home's pixels — a
    1024px tile, then jagged patches of #101010 over the film, changing as the
    controls came and went. A TextureView is drawn inside the window like
    everything else, so there is no hole to punch. It costs a copy per frame,
    which a 1080p stream on that box absorbs.

    The phone took it for the glass over the picture: a TextureView is drawn by
    the window, so Compose can record it and the controls can blur the video
    under them, the miniplayer's material (`pictureGlass`). Through a
    SurfaceView's hole there was nothing to sample, and the controls were paint
    — a clear disc with a rim, reported beside the miniplayer's dark glass.

    Only an XML attribute can choose the surface type, which is why this file
    exists at all.
-->
<androidx.media3.ui.PlayerView xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    app:surface_type="texture_view"
    app:use_controller="false" />
